===Etymology===
{{root|ru|ine-pro|*bʰreg-}}
{{bor+|ru|nl|broek}}. First attested in the eighteenth century, but became more popular a hundred years later. Ultimately from {{der|ru|gem-pro|*brōks}}, whence also {{cog|en|breeches}}, whence a doublet {{doublet|ru|бри́джи|notext=1}}. Further from {{der|ru|ine-pro|*bʰreg-}} (whence also {{cog|en|break}}, {{cog|la|frangō}}).

